# /users/12 → "next user" → /users/13, But the Page Still Shows User 12

## 1. What is it?

This is a route-reuse bug: the URL changes correctly (`/users/12` → `/users/13`), the router genuinely navigated, but the component on screen keeps rendering the *previous* user's data. It happens because the component that reads the route parameter reads it exactly once — in `ngOnInit`, off `route.snapshot` — and Angular reuses the same component instance across the two navigations instead of destroying and recreating it, so `ngOnInit` (and that one-time read) never runs again.

## 2. Why does this happen?

Angular's default `RouteReuseStrategy` treats "navigate from `/users/12` to `/users/13`" as **the same route being reactivated with different parameters**, not as leaving one route and entering another. Both URLs resolve to the identical route config (`path: 'users/:id'`, same component). Since the route itself doesn't change — only its parameter does — Angular's optimization is to keep the already-instantiated component alive, skip tearing it down and rebuilding it, and just update the `ActivatedRoute`'s parameter data feeding into it. This is intentional and usually desirable (it avoids destroying/recreating potentially expensive component trees, forms, subscriptions, etc. on every param change) — but it silently breaks any component that assumes "a new id means a new component instance means `ngOnInit` will run again."

## 3. How does it work?

**The buggy pattern** — reading the id once, in a lifecycle hook, off a snapshot:

```typescript
export class UserDetailComponent implements OnInit {
  private route = inject(ActivatedRoute);
  private userService = inject(UserService);
  user?: User;

  ngOnInit() {
    const id = this.route.snapshot.paramMap.get('id')!;
    this.userService.getUser(id).subscribe((u) => (this.user = u));
  }
}
```

`route.snapshot` is a frozen, point-in-time view of the route's state *at the moment it's read*. `ngOnInit` only fires once, when Angular first constructs the component. The very first time the user lands on `/users/12`, this all works — a fresh component is created, `ngOnInit` runs, `id` is `'12'`, the right user loads. But when they click "next user" and the router activates `/users/13` on the *same route config*, Angular reuses the existing `UserDetailComponent` instance. `ngOnInit` does not run again. The component just sits there, still holding the user-12 data it fetched once, while the URL bar and the rest of the app correctly report `/users/13`.

**The fix** — subscribe to the route's reactive parameter stream instead of reading a snapshot in a one-shot hook:

```typescript
export class UserDetailComponent implements OnInit {
  private route = inject(ActivatedRoute);
  private userService = inject(UserService);
  user?: User;

  ngOnInit() {
    this.route.paramMap
      .pipe(
        map((params) => params.get('id')!),
        distinctUntilChanged(),
        switchMap((id) => this.userService.getUser(id))
      )
      .subscribe((u) => (this.user = u));
  }
}
```

`route.paramMap` is an `Observable` tied to the lifetime of the `ActivatedRoute` itself, not to a single execution of `ngOnInit`. It emits every time the route's parameters change — including when Angular reuses the component instance and just swaps in new params. `ngOnInit` still only runs once (to set up the subscription), but the subscription itself keeps firing on every relevant navigation. `switchMap` is what makes this safe under fast repeated navigation: if the user clicks "next user" again before the previous `getUser()` call resolves, `switchMap` cancels the stale in-flight request so a slow, outdated response can never arrive late and overwrite the data for the id the user is actually looking at now. `distinctUntilChanged()` just skips a redundant re-fetch if the same id is emitted twice in a row.

**The detail that trips people up:** the fix is *not* "don't reuse the component" — it's "don't read route state in a hook that only runs once." The component instance being reused is fine and intentional; the bug was pairing a one-time read with a value that can change many times over that instance's life.

## 4. Example (exactly the code verified live in the StackBlitz demo above)

The demo puts the buggy and fixed versions side by side, each driven by its own named `<router-outlet>`, so both can be clicked independently while sharing the exact same route pattern and "Next user" trigger:

```typescript
// app.routes.ts — same path pattern, two outlets, so both panes are
// genuinely subject to the same route-reuse behavior being demonstrated
export const routes: Routes = [
  { path: 'users/:id', component: BuggyUserDetailComponent, outlet: 'buggy' },
  { path: 'users/:id', component: FixedUserDetailComponent, outlet: 'fixed' },
];
```

```typescript
